  "notes": "Amharic Ethiopic (Ge'ez) script. Abugida where each character encodes consonant + vowel; 7 vowel orders per consonant row. Ejectives: Tʼ, Čʼ, Pʼ, Tsʼ are phonemic. The 6th order (ə-vowel) is the inherent vowel. Ä, ʕ (Ayin) present in traditional script but may be realized as /ʔ/ or silent in colloquial speech. Stress exemption: Amharic has no phonemically contrastive word stress; prominence is weak/variable and is not marked by or predictable from the Ge'ez abugida modeled here; no stress block is declared. Source: Leslau, Reference Grammar of Amharic (1995). Coverage gap fix: the table originally covered only the seven basic vowel orders per consonant row and omitted three further groups that occur in running Amharic text and are attested in the wikipron/vox_communis gold — (1) the labialised Cʷa series written with dedicated Ethiopic glyphs (ሏ ሟ ሯ ሷ ቧ ቷ ቿ ኗ ኟ ዟ ዷ ጇ ጧ ፏ, plus the fuller velar/uvular Cʷ paradigm ቋ ቍ ኳ ጐ ጓ and the historical ħ-row labialised form ኋ), phonemic per Ado (2021); (2) the ቨ-series used to write loanword /v/; and (3) two historical letter rows that Ge'ez orthography still distinguishes but that merged in modern Amharic pronunciation — ኀ/ኃ/ኅ/ኸ/ኽ (originally a pharyngeal/velar fricative row, ħ or x) merged with /h/, and ፁ/ፃ/ፅ (the remaining columns of the archaic ፀ row) merged with the ejective affricate /tsʼ/ row (ጸ), matching the inherent-vowel member ፀ already mapped to tsʼə. The merger values are measured directly from wikipron gold (e.g. እኅት 'sister' -> ... h ɨ t, ቪዛ 'visa' -> v i z a, ኳስ 'ball' -> kʷ a s) rather than newly sourced, since no fetched primary source was found stating the merger explicitly; the labialisation values follow Ado (2021). The Ethiopic wordspace ፡ (and ። ፣) are punctuation (Unicode category Po), already stripped generically by the tokenizer, and are deliberately left unmapped rather than given a spec entry.",
